<h1>Which accessibility law applies to you, and since when</h1>
<p class="lede">The short answer: it depends on where you sell, not where you are. A US company selling to European consumers has to meet the European standard.</p>

<div class="prose">
  <h2>If you sell to consumers in the European Union</h2>
  <p>The <strong>European Accessibility Act</strong> applies, Directive (EU) 2019/882. It has been <strong>enforceable since 28 June 2025</strong>: it isn't an upcoming deadline, it's a date that has already passed.</p>
  <p>It binds you regardless of where your company is headquartered. What counts is that you offer the product or service to consumers inside the European Union.</p>
  <details>
    <summary>Which sectors and companies it covers</summary>
    <div>
      <p>It doesn't bind every company. The sectors covered are:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>E-commerce.</li>
        <li>Banking and payment services.</li>
        <li>Telecommunications and electronic communications.</li>
        <li>Audiovisual media services.</li>
        <li>Passenger transport: ticket sales, information, and terminals.</li>
        <li>E-books and e-readers.</li>
        <li>Computing equipment, phones, tablets, and self-service terminals such as ATMs and kiosks.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Microenterprise exemption:</strong> it covers only service providers with fewer than 10 employees and up to 2 million euros in annual turnover. Companies that manufacture or distribute covered products are not exempt, even as microenterprises.</p>
      <p>In Spain the transposition is <strong>Ley 11/2023</strong>, together with Royal Decree 193/2023. The penalty regime comes from Royal Legislative Decree 1/2013, with three tiers of infraction and fines ranging from €301 to €1,000,000.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <h2>If you sell in the United States</h2>
  <p>The <strong>ADA</strong> sets no technical standard in its text, but courts decide these cases using <strong>WCAG 2.1 Level AA</strong> as the de facto reference. In practice, that's the bar.</p>
  <p>There the risk arrives through the courts: the claimant is a private individual. The volume of web accessibility lawsuits grows year on year.</p>
  <details>
    <summary>Deadlines and the rules that do set a standard</summary>
    <div>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Section 508:</strong> binds federal agencies and those who sell to them.</li>
        <li><strong>ADA Title II:</strong> covers state and local public entities, with WCAG 2.1 AA as the required standard. The Department of Justice extended the deadlines by a year in April 2026: <strong>26 April 2027</strong> for populations of 50,000 or more, and <strong>26 April 2028</strong> for smaller ones.</li>
        <li><strong>ADA Title III:</strong> covers businesses open to the public. It has no published deadline or standard, and it's where the lawsuits concentrate.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The litigation figures circulating in the sector are published by accessibility vendors, not official sources. They're useful for the trend, not as exact numbers.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <h2>If you work with the Argentine State</h2>
  <p><strong>Ley 26.653</strong>, on the accessibility of information on web pages, applies. It has been in force since 2010, and the technical standards are set by the National Office of Information Technologies (ONTI).</p>
  <p><strong>It does not bind Argentine private companies in general.</strong> We'd rather say so before you check with your lawyer.</p>
  <details>
    <summary>Exactly who it binds</summary>
    <div>
      <ul>
        <li>The national State and its decentralized and autonomous bodies.</li>
        <li>State-owned companies and non-state public entities.</li>
        <li>Private companies holding public service concessions.</li>
        <li>Companies supplying goods and services to the State, or contracting with it.</li>
        <li>Civil society organizations receiving State subsidies or donations, or contracting with it.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>If your company is private and falls into none of those categories, Ley 26.653 doesn't bind you. That said, if you sell to European consumers, the EAA does.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <h2>The technical standard is the same in all three cases</h2>
  <p>All three frameworks point at the same guidelines. Europe through EN 301 549, which incorporates WCAG. US courts and the Department of Justice use WCAG 2.1 AA. ONTI takes them as its reference too.</p>
  <p>That means something concrete and in your favor: <strong>one audit against WCAG 2.2 serves all three frameworks</strong>. There's no need to audit three times or hire someone different in each country.</p>
</div>

